[Music]  i started  working at nasa in 1967 and i worked as  a computer  which is how we supported the engineers  then  and the apollo program was actually  starting when i got to nasa  i really enjoyed working with the  equations the equations that sometimes  that they brought into me  were very much like the equations that i  had worked on in graduate school  applied mathematics and i enjoyed  knowing what mathematics and those  equations do in the real world  most of the computers were female and  most of the engineers were male  the engineering division they were given  problems to solve so there was a little  bit of research  involved in what they did and there were  several  high-speed wind tunnels around the  building that we were in it was the  hypersonics area  and so they did research they gave  reports they gave  talks on the research that they were  doing they wrote papers  on the research that they were doing and  they published the papers  the women they did not do research they  basically did what the  engineers asked them to do and the women  did not get promoted  and it was very difficult to get your  name on a paper  after i'd been there several years i  said i wonder  why all of the women are assigned to the  computer office and all of the men  are assigned to the engineering office i  asked my supervisor about transferring  he says no i don't think  you could be able to do that and i  went back to my office i didn't like the  answer that much  i knew it was dangerous and i also knew  that if i went to somebody higher up  that  i could get fired that that they can  fire you for that  and i thought some more and and said  well you know what i think i still think  i should go to a higher up and  if if i get fired i'll go get another  job  and so that's what i did i  went to a director the head of  hypersonic aerodynamics  and i said mr becker i'd really like to  ask you a question if i might i said  i would like to know why a male and a  female coming here with pretty much the  same background  are put in such different positions when  they're here  and he looked at me and he says well you  know nobody has ever asked that question  before  and i said well i'm asking it now  you know and um pretty much that that  was  the end of the conversation and i went  back to my office  and again in about three weeks i  got promoted and i got transferred to  engineering  [Music]  i came to specialize in sonic boom  because when i was transferred to the  engineering section  sonic boom was a big issue in this world  we were supposed to be building a  commercial supersonic transport  but they had just canceled the program  because of the loudness of the sonic  boom  toulouse concourse one made her most  important public appearance back end  first  she was being nosed out of her hangar by  a tractor on the day for which britain  and france and the rest of the world  had waited so long the great giant  supersonic jetliner was going to fly  so now i say to the sst developers  i do not believe this nation will  tolerate  sonic booms and they had put a law on  the book that said there could be no  supersonic commercial flight across the  continental united states nasa says can  we reduce that sonic boom such this  doesn't happen  and i was put in a section that was  going to work on  trying to reduce the sonic bone  [Music]  i'm dr darden an engineer here at nasa  langley research center  i've done sonic boom research here for  many many years  what's a sonic boom doesn't it have to  do with the speed of airplanes  yes it does when an airplane travels  through the air it generates pressure  waves that travel at the speed of sound  in all directions  a plane flying at the speed of sound  moves at the same  speed as the disturbances causing a  shock wave to form  when the plane flies faster than the  speed of sound  the shock wave forms a cone shape  attached to the nose of the airplane  air pressure just inside the cone is  high and air pressure ahead of the cone  is normal this shock wave or cone  extends to the ground  perhaps 10 to 15 miles the sonic boom is  heard when the sudden change in pressure  crosses your ear  the young women who are who find out  that they really enjoy this they enjoy  what it does  i think you need to make sure that you  have learned  your math and your science well and you  speak up about  what you're doing and speak up about  what you want to do don't let  don't let yourself be bullied i think by  the young men in your class and things  like that  i understand that there are still a  number of young women who are leaving  engineering areas because  i guess there's a cross sometimes it's  from their classmates their male  classmates  sometimes apparently it's from some of  their male teachers  the four ps the perceive of yourself in  that job  plan what it is you have to do to get  there start preparing  that plan and persist  solve the problems you encounter and and  take a detour whatever it is you have to  do to not quit  keep going and persist in that job and  let it work out for you which is what  happened to me

